-- Version 7 → 8: a signed-in user's theme follows them.
--
-- One new table, nothing touched. See UiSettings.sq for what it holds and why
-- it is a key-value table rather than columns on `users`.
--
-- ── Why this needs no back-fill and no default ──────────────────────────────
--
-- An account with no row here has never chosen a theme, and the client already
-- has an answer for that: the shell's own defaults, which is exactly what every
-- session landed on before this migration existed. So the "empty" state after
-- this runs is indistinguishable from the state before it — nobody's screen
-- changes at the deploy, and the first person to touch the appearance control
-- is the first person to get a row.
--
-- That is the opposite of 6.sqm, whose column deliberately changed behaviour for
-- every existing account. The difference is that this is a preference and that
-- was a permission: a preference nobody has expressed should fall back to the
-- default, and a permission nobody has granted must not fall back to "allowed".
--
-- ── Why a plain CREATE TABLE is the whole migration ─────────────────────────
--
-- A new table with no dependents cannot invalidate anything that already exists:
-- no existing query names it, no existing row references it, and the foreign key
-- points from here to `users` rather than the other way about. That makes this
-- one of the rare schema changes where the migration and the declaration are
-- word-for-word the same statement — unlike 2.sqm, which had to rebuild a table
-- SQLite could not alter in place.
--
-- The DDL below is duplicated from UiSettings.sq, minus its comments, and must
-- stay in step with it. MigrationTest is what enforces that: it applies every
-- .sqm to the previous snapshot and compares the result, column for column and
-- foreign key for foreign key, against what the .sq files create from scratch.

CREATE TABLE user_ui_settings (
    user_id INTEGER NOT NULL,
    key TEXT NOT NULL,
    value TEXT NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, key),
    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);
